\chapter{Problem formulation}

In one sentence: given junctions, roads and cars find the best strategy for
controlling the junctions.  Definition of ``best'' will come later.  Since the
project was open-ended, it was needed to decide on the level of complexity
with which to work.  The problem eventually analyzed was, unsurprisingly, very
much of a toy problem.

\section{The (too) big problem}

Let's first look at the most general (more or less) formulation of the problem
to see what was omitted in the toy problem.  It should be able to represent
almost all aspects of traffic light control in real life, at the cost of being
impossible to analyze~\smiley.

Our world (``city'') consists of three main object types:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Cars that move around.  They can probably enter / leave the city at
    certain places.  At junctions they decide what turns they should
    take.\footnote {We assume that the choice of turns is independent from
    the delays on the road.  Strictly speaking this is not correct --- drivers
    try to stay away from jams they see ahead or hear about in traffic
    reports.  However dropping this assumption would make comparison between
    control strategies problematic.  For example if a person altered the order
    of his appointment to avoid a jam and thus lowered the total delay, it's a
    definite success of the reporting system but is it a success or a failure
    of the junction control system?}
      
  \item Unidirectional roads that delay cars driving through them.  The delay
    can depend on factors like amount of cars, random effects, etc.

  \item Junctions -- our question is \emph{How best to control them?}
\end{itemize}
A given junction has $M$ input roads and $N$ output roads.  There are
$R~\triangleq~MN$ routes for a car to pass through a junction (maybe less, for
example certain left turns can be forbidden, etc.).  A car arriving from input
direction $m$ and decides it wants to go to direction $n$ are appended to
queue $Q_{m,n}$ of the junction (not necessarily distinct -- different $n$s
can share the same queue).  At any moment the junction is serving zero or more
queues.  ``Serving a queue'' means that from time to time (typically at a
fixed rate for each queue, maybe after transition effects), one car (or more,
think multiple lanes), is popped from the queue (assuming it's not empty) and
forwarded to the target road(s) the car had chosen.

The choice of queue(s) to serve at a given moment is the subject of our
analysis.  Obviously it must be done under some constrains (otherwise we have
an ideal interchange where all queues can be served at once and no project to
do~\winkey):

\begin{itemize}
  \item Avoid collisions.  For every two routes $r \triangleq m \to n$, $r'
    \triangleq m' \to n'$ to pass the junction, let $C_{r,r'}$ be 1 if the
    these car paths collide, 0 if they don't.  Given this matrix we can find
    all \emph{maximum sets of compatible routes} (a set S is such if
    $\forall~r,r'~\in~S,\ C_{r,r'}=0$ but $\forall~\tilde r~\in~R-S\ \forall r
    in S,\ C_{\tilde r, r}=1$).

    Once computed (for given $C$), these sets redefine our task as choosing,
    at every point in time, which if these sets of queues to serve.

  \item Switching speed.  Instantaneous switching from serving one set of
    queues to another would cause collisions just as well.  So every switch is
    penalized with some dead time when nobody is served.  Let's call this dead
    time \emph{``yellow light''} even though it's not only it.  Similarly,
    acceleration from stopped state further reduces the throughput for some
    time after a starts to be served.  Actually, these effects happen per
    queue independent of other queues simultaneously served (which makes the
    above sets-of-queues switching model more complex).  The net effect: the
    lower the switching rate the higher the total car throughput.

  \item Pedestrians.  Every input and output path must be closed at least
    occasionally to allow for green lights for pedestrians too.  Since
    junction designers don't seem to care much for them, this is the last
    mention of them in this project~\smiley.
\end{itemize}

\section{What do we want?}

What are our criteria for measuring how ``good'' a given control policy is?

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Most importantly, we want minimum total \emph{delay} for a car along his
    way.  If road delays are constant this boils down to the delay in the
    junction queues.
    
  \item We also want \emph{stability} (predictable delays are better than
    strongly fluctuating ones) and \emph{fairness} (different cars should get
    similar treatment if possible).
\end{enumerate}

Since different cars travel in different ways, it makes more sense to measure
the average delay for each queue instead of for each car.  Given these delays,
we can try to define some effective delay of the city as some weighted
combination of these.

Stability and fairness can be represented by the variance of the delay
distribution.  Here it isn't really valid to use the per-queue measurement
approach if there is correlation between delays at different junctions.
However comparing cars traveling in different ways would inflate the variance
without reason.  The proper definition is to measure variances between all
cars the traveled the same routes (or at least shared route segments of some
length).  Since this would require tremendous simulation times to collect
enough data and since in the end no control strategies deliberately creating
correlation were used, the per-queue approach was used.  (Some care has to be
taken then when talking of the total variance over the city).

One more point to discuss: what input should be available to the control
system?  One can divide the research in this field into three somewhat
separate parts:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Given perfect information, develop the optimal control strategy.
  \item Check how sensitive the strategy is to imperfect information.
  \item Devise cheapest sensors giving maximum useful information.
\end{itemize}
This project belongs to the first part, so let's have full information~\smiley.

\section{Down to earth (the toy problem)}

The above model is way to complex to be useful.  Let's throw away complicating
details, until we get something manageable.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Cars decide where to turn statistically --- fixed known\footnote
    {\{60\% straight, 20\% right, 15\% left, 5\% reverse\} were used in the
    simulations.}  probabilities are assigned to the possible turns.  This
    turning profile doesn't closely model real traffic (for example cars will
    readily enter dead ends or go in circles).  The positive side is that an
    equal average car arrival rate at all junctions can easily be guaranteed.
    Thus we give up modeling of heterogeneous traffic load to be able to
    measure parameters as functions of the load.
  \item Get rid of multiple lanes --- all queues are served one car at a time
    with same constant speed.
  \item Ignore acceleration issues.  Use the ``vertical queue'' model --- the cars
    stack on on top of the other at the end of the road instead of stretching
    back from the stop line.  This is universally used in all traffic
    simulation literature I saw.
  \item This is close to discrete time, so let's adopt it (much easier to
    simulate, control, measure\ldots).  Implications: serve one car per
    cycle, roads length are integral number of cycles, etc.  One of the
    results is that at most one car per cycle can arrive from a every input
    road.
  \item Roads have constant delays (no jams along the roads, only at
    junctions; no ``platoon dispersion'' which is actually important to
    consider but we don't).
  \item No yellow lights.  The original motivation for this was that to be
    able to control multiple junctions in some correlated manner, we will want
    to limit the switching rate to about the speed of passing an average road
    anyway.  In the end only local control was implemented so this doesn't
    hold.  Yellow lights were actually implemented in the simulator but not
    used due to time limits.  Besides, the control strategies tried didn't
    take them into account anyway.  Timing constraints were modeled by
    directly placing lower/fixed limits on the time each queue is served.
  \item Serve only one queue at a time.  Analyzing serving of sets of queues
    simultaneously turned out to be too complex.  We do allow cars from one
    queue to serve many input directions (i.e. only one input road gets green
    light at the same time and cars can turn to all output directions).  Note
    that this is a big departure from reality!  It is suboptimal for typical
    junctions where most cars travel straight.  \emph{It limits the load} to
    be below $1/\max \{\mathrm{inputs}_J \mid J \in junctions\}$ because
    otherwise that junction will accumulate cars faster than it can dispose of
    them and will become unstable.  In our simulations this means that the
    load must be below $0.25$.
\end{enumerate}


\chapter{Project progress, issues and solutions}

\section{Theoretical analysis}

First I tried to analyze a single junction.  Consider the question of
determining the best green time proportions for a \emph{blind} (with no
sensors, doesn't even know which queues are empty) junction with known arrival
rates.  Assume the junction can serve a different queue every cycle (no
switching rate limits).

It knows only the arrival rates\footnote {note that a car can arrive together
with departing, so the effective rates are $\lambda(1-\mu)$ and
$\mu(1-\lambda)$.} $\{\lambda_m\}_{m=1}^M$ to the queues.  Assuming it
randomly choses the queue to serve each cycle with probabilities
$\{\mu_m\}_{m=1}^M$, how to chose those probabilities (satisfying
$\sum_{m=1}^M \lambda_m = 1$) to get minimal average delay?

This is a simple M/M/1 queues and optimization problem, resulting in:
\[
\mu_m = \lambda_n + \sqrt{\frac
  {\lambda_m(1-\lambda_m)}
  {\sum_{m=1}^M \lambda_m(1-\lambda_m)}}
\]
In other words, divide the extra serving rate $1-\sum \lambda_m$ proportionally
to the factors $\lambda_m(1-\lambda_m)$.  This was later simulated by
comparing junctions with optimal (according to the above formula) and
suboptimal $\mu_n$ probabilities and optimal junction indeed gave lower delay.

Then I tried to analyze a junction that can serve several queues at once.  This
was described before and as I said I gave it up.  Solving this for one
junction (still blind) seemed possible but very hard.  I realized that if I
ever want to reach multiple junctions, I must simplify the single junction.

I also considered the other details of the model (cycle length limits, yellow
light, etc.) and more or less settled for the model discussed above at this
stage.

Then I tried to consider non-blind junctions.  The problem is that the
probability to serve one queue depends (at least) on whether the other queues
are empty.  So I can't analyze queues independently.  Just the first 4 states
($(0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1)$) of the state diagram for two queues resulted in
a frightening diagram.  It's probably solvable with some smart trick but
becomes increasingly complex.

On the other hand I made a quick calculation of the probability that the above
optimal blind junction decides to serve an empty queue (thus wasting
throughput).  To my horror it turned out to exceed 50\% at some realistic
parameters!  So unless we only deal with heavy load (when queues seldom become
empty), a blind junction is a terrible waste.

The conclusion from all this was that the time came to leave theory aside and
write a computer program for simulation.

I also briefly looked at predicting which cars have good chances of being
stuck at next junction and giving them lower priority (since serving them
later probably won't increase their total delay).  I didn't find an obviously
good algorithm and dropped the issue deciding that I better implement at least
something without prediction.

\section{Software}

\subsection{Implementation language}

I've chosen Python that I recently learned and came to like very much.  It
seemed well suited to the task at hand.  From its \file{README}:

\begin{quote}
\textbf{What is Python anyway?}

Python is an interpreted object-oriented programming language suitable
(among other uses) for distributed application development,
scripting, numeric computing and system testing.  Python is often
compared to Tcl, Perl, Java, JavaScript, Visual Basic or Scheme.  To
find out more about what Python can do for you, point your browser to
http://www.python.org/.
\end{quote}

Was it worth it?  Sure!  I feel that the high abstraction level and quick
development cycle Python provided has paid off many times.  This project required
many small tasks having to do with organizing the simulation runs and such a
scripting language is perfect for them.

The performance became a bottleneck at the simulation running period but still
I think it was worth the development ease.  Besides the task is mostly
symbolic computations and languages like C aren't that fast at them anyway.

\subsection{Architecture}

The problem at hand nicely lends itself to an object-oriented architecture (at
which Python is very good, in my opinion; among others multiple inheritance
was heavily used).

A good way to get a bird's eye view of the architecture is to describe the
function of the modules composing the program:

\begin{description}
  \item[utils] Random functions (pun intended, one of them deals with
    random numbers).
    
  \item[textgrid] Simple text formatting facility for printing grid
    pictures.
    
  \item[simtime] Registry objects for city elements having
    \meth{.tick()} methods that call them every cycle (in partially defined
    order).
    
  \item[connect] Facilities for defining and conveniently connecting
    elements that have one or more named inputs and/or outputs.
    
  \item[basic] Basic elements with one input and/or output:
    \class{Car}, \class{Source}s that produce cars, \class{Drain}s that
    consume them, \class{Road}, \class{Queue}s and simplest \class{Server}s.
    
  \item[multi] Complex elements with many inputs and/or outputs:
    support base classes, \class{RandomRouter}s and \class{SwitchedServer}.
    
  \item[city] Almost the top module, imports the rest.  Implement
    \class{City} objects that contain elements and \class{GridPlan} objects
    that represent subset-of-grid city geometries and can implement them by
    creating cities.
    
  \item[run] Top file running the whole thing.  Contains all the
    parameter choosing, running, logging and graphing logic.

  \item[mktable] A stand-alone script that parses log files, treats them as
    multidimensional information and prepares beautiful \LaTeX tables with
    different views of it (with value selection and averaging abilities).
\end{description}

\subsection{Highlights}

Here are some highlights of problems and their solutions in the simulator.

\begin{itemize}
  \item Cars are passive object carried around by elements of the city.  Their
    only methods are for statistics collecting.

  \item Proper ordering of actions was achieved by:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Procedural means in the simplest cases (for example injecting a
        car into some element is done by calling its \meth{.append(car)}
        method which can react to this immediately).
      \item The long-term time line is managed by a \class{SimTime} object
        (every city has one).  Most elements have a \meth{.tick()} method.
        The time manager calls this method of all objects that were registered
        with it every cycle.  To make interactions during the same cycle
        well-defined, the objects are divided into several ``phases'' by their
        class and called with certain order between the phases (compare with
        ``delta'' times in VHDL simulation).
    \end{itemize}
    
  \item Connecting elements is based on a \class{Group} interface (implemented
    by all elements) of an object which has named\footnote {A default name is
    used for single input/output cases} input and/or output ports.  Connecting
    resolves input/output names between two objects (like linking programs)
    and returns a virtual \class{Group} combining the unresolved ports of both
    objects.  Virtual \class{Group}s are also used to implement renaming ports
    and ``overlaying'' objects without linking.  These features proved very
    useful in the creation of complex cities.

  \item Junctions were implemented as consisting of two parts: a
    \class{SwitchedServer} with multiple inputs and one output connected to a
    \class{RandomRouter} with one input and multiple outputs.  The serves
    serves at most one queue at a time.  The router looks at the original
    direction recorded in the car by the server to choose probabilities for
    the possible outputs.  There are also many slave objects to represent the
    queues and outputs.

    In the server, unlike most other objects in the program, I wasn't
    satisfied with sub-classing the base class to define the possible control
    strategies (even with multiple inheritance) because it didn't provide
    enough flexibility.  Instead, a plug-ins-based architecture was employed.
    There is one \class{SwitchedServer} class that is parametrized with a list
    of filters.  They are applied in order, starting with the full list of
    input queues, until one or zero candidates remain.

    This way, the control strategy specification was split into many
    components, mostly orthogonal:
    \begin{description}
      \item[rand\_filter] Break ties by choosing a random queue.
      \item[lru\_filter] Break ties by choosing the least-recently-used queue.
        If previous filters (if any) result in stable queues set, this results
        in a round-robin behavior.
      \item[nonempty\_filter] Don't serve empty queues.
      \item[hold\_filter] Allow deciding on a queue only once in $k$ cycles.
      \item[attempt\_hold\_filter] Serve same queue that was served last time,
        unless it was dropped by previous filters (i.e. switch queue only when
        forced).  In combination with hold\_filter, allows to prolong serving
        of one queue to more than $k$ cycles.
      \item[longest\_queue\_filter] Serve the queue with most cars.
      \item[last\_emptying\_filter] Serve queues predicted to empty last (if
        served continuously), based on input load measurements done by each
        queue.  Queues measuring load by sliding window and exponential
        averaging were implemented\footnote {only exponential averaging was
        tried in simulations, with characteristic decay length of 4 cycles; it
        didn't make much difference anyway}.  For estimated arrival rate
        $\lambda$ and serving rate $1$, a queue of length $L$ is expected to
        become empty after $L / (1 - \lambda)$ cycles.
      \item[inorder\_filter] Serve the queue whose first car waited most.
        This was intended to be the most fair strategy theoretically possible
        by effectively treating all queues as one.  Note that it is almost
        completely impossible to implement in real life.  Among others, it
        doesn't have this property when switching rate is limited; however
        switching to a different queue each car is very impractical\ldots
    \end{description}
    These filters can be combined in various creative ways; many but not all
    of them were exercised by the simulations.

    Yellow light was implemented in the \class{SwitchedServer} class itself
    (it didn't fit the filters model conveniently enough) but as already said
    it wasn't used in simulations (it wasn't even tested).
        
  \item ``Interesting'' city geometries were generated without dealing with
    arbitrary graphs by starting with a full rectangular grid , dropping a
    certain portion (30\% in the simulation) of the roads and treating
    junctions according to number of roads: 3 or 4 -- remains a junction, 2 --
    join the road into longer roads, 1 -- a source/drain pair and for 0
    nothing is created.  All roads are bi-directional.  Example:

    \begin{center}
      % Ugly size hacks ahead.  Don't know to make better now...
      \begin{minipage}[c]{2cm}
        \begin{verbatim}
+-O-O-O-+
| | | | |
O-O-O-O-O
| | | | |
O-O-O-O-O
| | | | |
O-O-O-O-O
| | | | |
+-O-O-O-+
        \end{verbatim}
      \end{minipage}
      {\Huge $\Rightarrow$}
      \begin{minipage}[c]{2cm}
        \begin{verbatim}
