        \end{verbatim}
      \end{minipage}
    \end{center}
    (where \txt{-} and \txt{+} represent roads, \txt{+} long roads join,
    \txt{O} junctions and \txt{@} source/drain pairs).

  \item Directions were represented with vectors -- tuples of $(\delta x,
    \delta y)$.  This proved much more useful then encoding them with, say
    $\{0,1,2,3\}$, because they could be directly manipulated in many creative
    ways.
    
  \item To make comparisons between different strategies fair, the city
    geometry is generated in a plan object once and multiple cities can be
    instantiated based on the same plan.

  \item When running, progress and results are both printed to the screen and
    logged to a file too using a file-like \class{Log} class.  Simple graphs
    are with a s function that writes \file{*.plt} and referred by them
    \file{*.dat} files and runs \file{gnuplot} to do the work.
    
  \item Since the run was very long (5 hours first part, above 2 days the
    second), I needed a way to stop and restart where I left.   To this end:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item City plans ascii-art dumps (in the above format) are written into
        \file{*.plan} files in the output directory; if the file already
        exists, it's loaded and the plan is set from it instead of being
        randomly generated.
      \item When the log file is opened for appending, it's also scanned for
        headers of simulations already run.  Before running every simulation,
        it's compared to the loaded list and skipped if already done.
    \end{itemize}
    These features made the annoyingly long runs quite
    tolerable\ldots\footnote {Also, the just-in-time optimizer for Python,
    Psyco (\texttt{http://psyco.sf.net}) was used and seemed to give a speedup
    of somewhat below 2, lower than its average performance but still very
    welcome \smiley}

  \item Simulation parameters are defined with nested loops over different
    aspects of the parameters.  Every aspect's possible values have short
    symbolic names which, combined into one string, are used to name output
    files and identify the parameter sets in graphs and logs.  For example:
    \txt{0.20-hold4\_fix\_rand-C}.

  \item ``Visualization'' of numbers in nice tables was implemented in a
    separate log$\to$tables converter script which knows to nest tables to
    show more than 2-dimensional data.
\end{itemize}

You are referred to the source code for the full details.  The source code
contains pretty good documentation that should be enough for understanding and
modifying the code.  It does not contain enough documentation for interfacing
to the code without reading it, simply because it provides no such
interface~\smiley.  There is no clear implementation/user division line.
Levels of convenience were implemented, until I was able to run the thing
conveniently (with the Python prompt as my interface).  If you just want to
reproduce my results, its enough to say at the Python prompt:
\begin{verbatim}
  >>> from run import *
  >>> all_react()
  >>> all_main()
\end{verbatim}
(be prepared for a couple days of running!).  However if you want alter
simulation parameters, you need to modify the code --- from superficial
\module{run.py} modifications to writing new classes, depending on how much you
want to change.


\chapter{Simulation Results and Conclusions}

\section{First Part -- Probing the Ground}

The main purpose of the first serious run was to find out good parameters for
subsequent runs.

One issue that interested me was the validity of averaging data over some time
that includes transition effects in the beginning, until the initially empty
city stabilizes around a given number of cars.  This was checked by simulating
cities and recording the number of cars at every moment.  It was later plotted
(see Figure~\vref{fig-car} for example) so that the reaction time to turning
the sources on at $t=0$ can be visually inspected.  Similarly, at $t=4000$
cycles, all sources were turned off and simulation continued until $t=6000$;
the reaction time to this too can be inspected.

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{results/react_0.10-rand-car.ps}
  \caption{Reaction test -- car number: load=0.10, free, rand}
  \label{fig-car}
\end{figure}

The second thing checked was how long do I need to run to get sensible
results.  This was visualized by plotting the average and deviation over the
whole city, accumulated from $t=0$ as function of $t$ (see
Figures~\vrefrange{fig-wait}{fig-dev} for example).

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{results/react_0.10-hold_rand-wait.ps}
  \caption{Reaction test -- average delay: load=0.10, hold=4, rand}
  \label{fig-wait}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{results/react_0.10-hold_rand-dev.ps}
  \caption{Reaction test -- total deviation: load=0.10, hold=4, rand}
  \label{fig-dev}
\end{figure}

The third thing checked was how big a city is needed.  Every set of parameters
was tried on 3 cities of sizes 5\x5, 10\x10 and 15\x15.  Each of the above
plots was combined on one sheet for the three cities.

Last but not least, the volume of the run wasn't small.  It ran for about 5
hours\footnote {All run times are reported from my 933MHz Pentium III}.  All
of the above was repeated for 3 load values (0.05, 0.10, 0.20\footnote {Recall
that 0.25 would saturate the system}) and a fair number of control policies.
The collected statistics are instructive on their own.

\subsection{Results}

First, the qualitative conclusions:
\begin{itemize}
  \conc The number of cars typically stabilizes after no more than 1000
    cycles.  This doesn't seem to contribute much to the final values -- the
    curve becomes flat.  Conclusion: \emph{it seems OK to include the startup
    period in measurement}.
  \conc After 5000 cycles the resulting delay and deviation become as stable
    as they can get.  Since the deviation doesn't go down, we know this is the
    ``inherent'' deviation of the system and result from low amount of
    measurement.  \emph{5000 cycles will be used for second part of
    measurements.}
  \conc The 10\x10 and 15\x15 cities differ little one from the other but
    differ seriously from 5\x5.  The problem with 5\x5 is that it is too small
    for reliable statistics.  For example I saw frequently 5\x5 cities torn
    into two halves, etc.  \emph{To be on the safe side, 15\x15 will be used
    for the second part.}
\end{itemize}

The numerical results are summed up in Table~\vref{tab-react}, based on short
identifiers describing different aspects of the run parameters.  There is the
load of course; there is also the plan name (written last).  The rest
describes the control policy: \txt{hold} means that switching was limited to
once in 4 cycles (in this part), \txt{lru}/\txt{rand} are the blind policies,
\txt{longest}, \txt{hist}, \txt{inorder} are the queue-inspecting policies
described before.  \txt{avg} and \txt{dev} are obviously the average and the
deviation.

Analysis of the numerical results:
\begin{itemize}
  \conc We see, as can be easily expected, that the three smart policies
    (which all don't serve empty queues) have almost equal delay.  This makes
    sense -- the average delay depends only on the portion of throughput
    wasted by serving empty queues.  The differences between them are very
    small and don't seem systematic so they should be attributed to random
    variations.
  \conc We see that LRU and random serving are both much worse but while LRU
    is still in some acceptable bounds, the random policy gives completely
    horrible results.  It can be intuitively expected that LRU which cover
    queues more systematically, will leave less cars to wait for long;
    nevertheless the fact that \emph{blind LRU's delay is closer to that of
    controls with empty queue sensors} is very surprising to me.  I'm not sure
    how to explain that big difference.
  \conc We see that the blind policies have bigger deviations than the ones
    with sensors.  This is reasonable, especially since the values are all on
    the positive side of the zero (so typical probability distributions have
    give higher deviations with high higher means).
  \conc We see that the in-order serving policy gives smaller deviation than
    all others.  This is consistent with the predictions -- it was designed
    precisely for that -- to result in lowest theoretically possible
    deviation.
  \conc The longest queue policy and the arrival rate measuring policy give
    practically equal results.  Actually they are very similar: the later just
    divides the queue length by $1 - \lambda$.  Since in our simulations the
    load is approximately equal on all inputs, we see no big difference.
    Conclusion: \emph{There is no strong autocorrelation in the arrival rates
    in this system} so the measurements of the past give little information
    about the future.
  \conc The results with switching rate limited to once per 4 cycles are quite
    similar, except for the overall higher delay and waiting time.  This is
    not very surprising but not obvious either -- the ``smart'' policies can't
    be expected any longer to give equal delays.  Indeed they don't but the
    results are not dramatically conclusive.

    Roth policies based on the queue length give very close results.  This is
    a bit unexpected since the with limited switching rate, the arrival rate
    should have a certain autocorrelation.  The probable explanation is that
    the measurement scheme which has a reaction time of the same order as the
    autocorrelation time, (add the road delay to it) is reacting too late.
    \emph{Communication between junctions should be able to provide an
    advantage here}.

    There are noticeable differences between the in-order policy and the
    queue-length based policies.  They are surprising: for very low
    loads\footnote {To get a feeling: with switching every 4 cycles and
    arrival rate of 0.05, less than one car arrives on average during each red
    light period!}, serving the car waiting the most is \emph{not only more
    fair but also seems to have a predictive quality} for the future beyond
    that given by the current queue length!  On the other hand for higher
    loads, serving in order of arrival starts to be worse than looking at the
    queue length.

    On a second thought it makes sense: when queues rarely exceed a length of
    1 car, the arrival time is an extra information that is not available to
    the length-of-queue based strategy.  However the length of the queue is
    much more solid promise for less serving of empty queues~--- when there is
    a difference in length it's more important than arrival times of the first
    cars in each queue\ldots
\end{itemize}

\begin{sidewaystable}
  \begin{center}
    \input{react.tex}
    \caption{Reaction test numerical results, averaged over 10\x10 \& 15\x15
      cities.}
    \label{tab-react}
  \end{center}
\end{sidewaystable}

\section{Second Part -- the Heavy Car Crunching}

With the experience from the first run described above, a more heavy and
exhaustive simulation was run, with the following parameters:

\begin{itemize}
  \item 15\x15 cities.
  \item 3 different plans of this size (called \txt{A}, \txt{B} and \txt{C})
    were simulated.  This is intended to determine what fluctuations derive
    from the random city geometry and what is defined by the other parameters.
  \item Each run is for 5000 cycles.
  \item Higher load values: [0.15, 0.2, 0.23].  The loads considered
    previously are a bit too low.  We wanted to cover these loads too.
  \item More control policies.  All combinations of the following\footnote
    {except for \txt{blind} combined with \txt{flex} which would serve one
    queue indefinitely\ldots} were tried:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item \txt{free}, \txt{hold2}, \txt{hold4}, \txt{hold4} --- Switching
        rate is unlimited or limited to once per 2, 4 or 8 cycles.
        
      \item \txt{flex} --- Serve same queue as last time, if possible.  In
        combination with \txt{hold}$k$, also means that the switching rate
        limit will be flexible -- if the queue is not empty after $k$ cycles,
        continue serving it.
        
        \txt{fix} --- the opposite.  Note that the same queue can still be
        re-selected for another $k$ cycles -- but only on $t \bmod k = 0$
        boundaries.
        
      \item \txt{blind} --- Proceed directly to LRU/random resolution below.

        \txt{nonempty} --- Only try to serve non-empty queues; no preference
        beyond that.

        \txt{longest} --- Choose the queue(s) with most cars.

        \txt{hist} --- Choose the queue(s) predicted to become empty last if
        served continuously.

        \txt{inorder} --- Choose the queue(s) whose cars arrived first.

      \item \txt{rand} --- If the above selection left several equally good
        queues, resolve by choosing at random.

        \txt{lru} --- Resolve ties in LRU manner.
    \end{itemize}
    These short names will be used extensively in the following discussions of
    the results.

  \item Separate statistics for junctions with 3 and 4 roads.  The motivations
    is that given equal average load at all roads, junctions with 4 roads
    experience higher total load than ones with 3 roads.  This can be
    responsible for part of the deviation.  Collecting statistics separately
    will not suffer from this ``false'' deviation.
\end{itemize} 

These parameters combine to amount for a very long run.  There are 72
different policies; multiplied by 3 load values and 3 plans, we get 648 runs.
The sum of the counts of cars that passed through each junction (i.e. each car
is counted many times) is 207,140,557 (two hundred millions) \smiley.  The
total running time exceeded 48 hours (I don't have exact figures because
thanks to the restart-ability facilities, I was able to interrupt for
rebooting to a lesser OS several times).

\subsection{Results}

The data from this run is essentially 8-dimensional.  Displaying all of it at
once is impossible and incomprehensible.  So different projections/cuts of it
will be presented, with arbitrary choices (or averaging where sensible)
imposed on parameters that give less insight to the issues at hand, in order
to reduce the number of dimensions.

\begin{itemize}
  
  \conc Table \vref{tab-main34} compares between statistics for junctions with
    3 and 4 roads.  It clearly shows that the suspicions were true.
    \emph{There is a large difference between the statistics over junctions
    having 3 and 4 roads}, because they have different load.  The lesson to
    take from this is that for precise measurements, they should not be mixed.
    So, in most following checks, I chose to concentrate on junctions with 4
    roads (we wanted higher loads after all).
    
  \begin{table}
    \begin{center}
      \input{main34.tex}
      \caption{Comparison of junctions with 3 and 4 roads, averaged over
        plans, selected parameters: \txt{hold2}, \txt{fix}, \txt{lru}}
      \label{tab-main34}
    \end{center}
  \end{table}

  \begin{table}
    \begin{center}
      \input{mainplans3.tex}
      \caption{Comparison of the 3 plans, selected parameters: \txt{hold2},
        \txt{fix}, \txt{lru}, \txt{3} roads}
      \label{tab-mainplans3}
    \end{center}
  \end{table}

  \begin{table}
    \begin{center}
      \input{mainplans4.tex}
      \caption{Comparison of the 3 plans, selected parameters: \txt{hold2},
        \txt{fix}, \txt{lru}, \txt{4} roads}
      \label{tab-mainplans4}
    \end{center}
  \end{table}

  \conc Now let's check the dependence on a specific plan.  Tables
    \vrefrange{tab-mainplans3}{tab-mainplans4} compares the 3 city plans, for
    junctions with 3 and 4 roads respectively (in this case the results were
    interesting enough to include both of them).  There are definite and quite
    systematic differences.

    For 3 roads (all loads) or 4 with the lower load of 0.15, the ordering of
    delays A $>$ B $>$ C is observed without exception.  Interestingly, in all
    cases but one B has the highest deviation.  But now \emph{there is a
    breaking point on the \textsl{load\x{}number-of-roads} scale} at which
    the relation between the city plans changes.

    For 4 roads with the higher loads 0.20 and 0.23, the trend changes.  B has
    the highest delay with no exceptions; it also has the highest deviation
    except for one case.  A and C are quite close except for the blind case
    which has really big delays and differences; there doesn't seem to be a
    definitive ordering between them.

    The question is, what properties of a city plan make it have overall
    higher or lower average/deviation?  I must admit I found no good answer to
    this.  I counted the components --- sources/drains, long roads, junctions
    with 3/4 roads and total junctions --- of the city plans:
    \begin{center}
      \begin{tabular}{c||r|r|r|r|r|r}
          & 0 & 1  & 2  & 3  & 4  & 3+4 \\
        \hline \hline
        A & 3 & 16 & 73 & 96 & 37 & 133 \\
        \hline
        B & 3 & 30 & 78 & 76 & 38 & 114 \\
        \hline
        C & 9 & 21 & 68 & 89 & 38 & 127 \\
      \end{tabular}
    \end{center}
    None of these seems to explain directly the A $>$ B $>$ C ordering
    observed for lower loads.  However one thing does stand out: B has the
    highest number of sources/drains and highest number of road continuations,
    at the expense of junctions.  How can this explain B's deviation being the
    highest?  The more sources/drains, the shorter is the average car's
    presence in the city --- but this shouldn't matter directly because
    measurements are per queue.  Another difference is that incoming car
    streams have less autocorrelation (our sources emit white noise).\footnote
    {Recall that these tables are for switching speed of once in 2 cycles,
    which imposes at least some autocorrelation on the streams of cars emitted
    by junctions.}  Road lengths shouldn't have direct effect on the control
    policies described.  So, without proof, I can \emph{guess: cars randomly
    entering/leaving the roads lower the autocorrelation of the car streams in
    the system which has adverse effect on the deviations}.\footnote
    {Prediction: ``platoon dispersion'' on real-life roads probably has
    similar effect.}

    In the face of these differences between cities, to what extent can we
    trust other information extracted from the data?  First, can we bound the
    differences due to the plan?  No, not tightly enough.  The differences in
    many cases are as high as one third!  However, we are saved by the fact
    that, as can be seen from the tables, the relations between different
    parameters are very similar for all three city plans.  So while
    \emph{absolute results for given cities are not representative, relative
    results are probably reliable}.  From now on I will average results
    between the 3 plans.

  \begin{sidewaystable}
    \begin{center}
      \input{mainflex.tex}
      \caption{Comparison of switching speeds, fixed and flexible, selected
        parameters: \txt{0.20} load, \txt{lru}, \txt{4} roads, averaged over
        plans.  Note that \txt{flex} with \txt{blind} makes no sense (it would
        be stuck on one queue indefinitely).}
      \label{tab-mainflex}
    \end{center}
  \end{sidewaystable}

  \conc Now, let's consider the effects of different switching speeds, both
    fixed and flexible.  Table \vref{tab-mainflex} shows them for one load
    (0.20), with LRU tie resolving.  The first thing to see is that for most
    cases, flex gives lower times (by up to $\approx 20\%$).  This makes
    sense.  The average delay should only depend on the frequency of serving
    empty queues.  For any non-blind policy with fixed switching rate $k$,
    this can only happen on cycles $2\ldots k$ out of every $k$ cycles.  For a
    flexible switching rate, if the period of serving a queue is prolonged by
    $l$ cycles, a non-empty queue can be served again only on cycles $2\ldots
    k$ out of the $k+l$ cycles.  So \emph{flexibly prolonging green time when
    the queue still has cars lowers the average delay by lowering the
    opportunity of serving empty queues}.  The same can be expected of flexibly
    shortening the serving time.  Indeed, this is observed: the average delays
    drops with the reduction of the minimal green time.  However, \emph{with
    no minimal green time limit (\txt{free}), trying to prolong the green time
    is not efficient}~--- \txt{fix} gives lower delay than \txt{flex}.  I
    don't know why exactly this happens --- for \txt{free} empty queues are
    never served so the delay depends only on complex second-order timing
    interactions between junctions\ldots\footnote {By these I mean the
    correlations between car arrivals from different directions to the same
    junction; understanding them falls beyond the scope of this project.}

    Another effect is that \txt{flex} results in higher deviations.  This is
    expected --- it serves the same queue longer, making other queues wait
    more.

  \begin{sidewaystable}
    \begin{center}
      \input{mainwisdoms.tex}
      \caption{Comparison of control policies for different loads, selected
        parameters: \txt{fix}, \txt{lru}, \txt{4} roads, averaged over plans.}
      \label{tab-mainwisdoms}
    \end{center}
  \end{sidewaystable}

  \conc Finally, table \vref{tab-mainwisdoms} compares the different control
    policies, for different loads and switching rates.  The results are
    similar to the ones from the first (reaction speed) runs; I won't repeat
    all the previous conclusions here.  The main difference is that higher
    loads were used, so the results are more fluctuating.  In particular, even
    for \txt{free} the results are noticeable (but not critical) differences
    between the non-blind policies.  This can be attributed to second-order
    interactions.

    We see that \emph{with the increase of load, the differences between the
    policies don't seem to become smaller}.  In particular, \txt{blind}
    remains very ineffective.  This was unexpected --- I would think that when
    most queues are full, a blind junction ``has no choice'' but to be
    effective too \smiley.  Probably the loads are still not high enough ---
    with the smart policies, cars wait up to 4 cycles on average, which means
    1-2 cars per queue in steady state\ldots

    We see that \emph{with stronger limits on the switching rate, all policies
    become more similar}.  This is expected --- none of them is can predict
    the future very well so they all are more or less blind with respect to
    it.

    Again, the differences between \txt{hist} and \txt{longest} are very small
    and unsystematic; considering the fluctuations in the results, there is no
    statically meaningful difference between them.  Under the conditions of
    these simulations, \emph{measuring the incoming load of each queue doesn't
    prove useful} (or maybe the information wasn't put to good use).

    Continuing the trend from the first runs, \txt{inorder} lags behind them
    and its delay isn't different from \txt{nonempty}.  Serving in order of
    arrival doesn't improve the delay, except under the smallest load.  However
    it does improve the deviation better than any other method.  Here is a
    clear \emph{delay/fairness trade-off}.

    The rest is consistent with the conclusions from the first run.  There are
    surely points I missed in the analysis and other interesting tables to
    produce but I have to stop somewhere.  You are invited to mine the results
    further \winkey.
    
\end{itemize}

\section{Bottom lines and future directions}

First, avoid serving empty queues as much as possible.  If you can't switch
the served queue every cycle (e.g. in real life), choosing the longest queue
is a good strategy to minimize it.  For very low loads, when several queues
have equal length, breaking the tie by choosing the one whose front car
arrived first should be better yet.

Second, flexibly controlling the green time is another way to reduce it.
Serve a queue as long as it has cars, when it empties, switch to another.  The
lower the minimum green time limit, the better (unless you are penalized by
yellow light time).  A limit on maximum green time would be useful if you
don't want to harm fairness.

Third, always prefer least-recently-used approaches to picking at random.
This helps both for delay and fairness.  There is some trade-off between delay
and fairness.  Serving in order of arrival is always most fair\footnote {For
  \txt{free}, it is a special case of the PGPS weighted fair queuing
  algorithm, for equal $\phi_i$ weights.}.  However it is not very practical
since for \txt{hold} it gives suboptimal delay.

There are many ways to continue this work --- it's just the tip of the
iceberg.  One could perform more research with the current simulator.  In
particular, the conditions tried were still not representative of real-life
traffic; higher loads, longer green times, longer roads and yellow lights
would be interesting to explore.  The role of the city's geometry should be
explored more; the current Manhattan-like geometry is very arbitrary.

It would be interesting to implement logging/visualization facilities that
would allow one to track the history of a single car (or a little group) and
get a feeling of the system's behavior.  This could help to gain intuition for
second-order effects.

Smarter car routing would be interesting; a simple approach is to choose a
route like now when a car is created and remove all cycles from it.

Each of the assumptions described at the first chapter can be considered for
lifting.  Serving multiple queues at once would be most valuable for getting
closer to reality; unfortunately it will probably severely complicate all
control policies.

The ultimate goal is probably devising control policies that involve
communication between junctions.

Use your imagination to continue this list\ldots



